-- title:
--   Stabilizer degree budget with explicit cubic bases in both charts
-- statement:
--   This is the remaining stabilizer degree-budget theorem with explicit cubic bases in both charts.
--
--   Keep all hypotheses and conclusions of the previous frontier: the period pair, sigma data, entire coordinates, quasiperiod map, bidegree $(m,n)$, finite set $X$ containing zero, nonzero pullback, and order at least $3U+1$ on the triple sumset. The constant must be uniform in $m,n,U,X,Q$.
--
--   One must still choose a nonempty zero locus $W$, an exponent $b\le2$, and natural multiplicity budgets $e_c$ for the represented cosets modulo its linear-translation image. For each represented coset, choose an available chart point in $X+X+X$ and bound every qualifying persistent minimal prime's localized length by $e_c$.
--
--   The base in either chart is now the explicit ideal
--
--   $$I_c=(F_c)+(Q_c).$$
--
--   The persistent primes are minimal over the differential prolongations at both $iU$ and $(i+1)U$, for $i\in\{0,1,2\}$. The original sum bound remains unchanged:
--
--   $$\sum_c e_c\le C\begin{cases}m,&\text{all linear translation directions have zero additive coordinate},\\1,&\text{otherwise}\end{cases}n^b.$$
--
--   Only the budget data type is changed in the formal statement. The complete ideal-identification theorem and a checked converse preserve every candidate condition and localized quotient length. Global geometric gluing as needed, locus and chart-point selection, the coset/component connection and the uniform total-length degree estimate remain obligations.
-- source:
--   Philippon (1986), Lemmes de zeros dans les groupes algebriques commutatifs, Bull. Soc. Math. France 114, 355-383, section 5, pp. 380-382: ambient defining ideals in the global zero-estimate argument. https://www.numdam.org/item/10.24033/bsmf.2060.pdf. Senthil Kumar K (2026), Appendix A, Theorem A.2. https://doi.org/10.1017/S001309152610145X. Building on the Proved first-chart relation ideal, the new argument identifies the second chart kernel by rational coordinate transition, denominator clearing and exact saturation of its cubic ideal. Both bases are now generated by the explicit cubic and normalized auxiliary polynomial. The budget conditions and all localized lengths are preserved. Full geometric gluing, locus and coset/component selection, and the uniform total-degree estimate remain outside this result.

